// All examples verified on morloc 0.100.2 (2026-09-02).
// Corrections in this pass:
//  - the {cpp} helper was named `default`, which is a C++ KEYWORD; the snippet
//    does not compile (`error: expected unqualified-id before 'default'`).
//    Renamed to `orDefault` and verified with g++ -std=c++20.
//  - `[source, c++]` -> `[source, cpp]`.
//  - added real output for every example, including the `--keep-null` pair and
//    the nested-optional collapse.

=== Optional types

Every language needs a way to say "no value here". Query a database for a row
that does not exist, or read a parameter that was never set, and something has
to come back. Python has `None`, R has `NULL`, JSON has `null`, and {cpp} solves
it in the library with `std::optional<T>`.

Morloc's principle is that sourced functions stay idiomatic, so it needs a
mechanism that lowers to each language's own answer while staying consistent
across the boundary. That is what the optional type is for.

==== Syntax

The `?` prefix marks a type as optional, and `Null` is the absent value. `?Int`
is an integer that might be absent, `?Str` a string that might be. The prefix
applies to any type, including lists (`?[Int]`) and records (`?Person`).

[source, morloc]
----
--' Get the first element of a list, or nothing
safeHead :: [Int] -> ?Int

testNull :: ?Int
testNull = Null
----

NOTE: Morloc writes `Null` capitalized in source, following the convention that
constructors start with an uppercase letter -- the same as `True` and `False`.
In JSON output it serializes as lowercase `null`, per the JSON standard.

==== Working with optional values

Functions that produce or consume optionals are sourced like any others:

[source, morloc]
.main.loc
----
module main (testSafeHead, testSafeHeadEmpty, testFromNull)

import root-py

safeHead :: [Int] -> ?Int
safeHead xs
  ? length xs == 0 = Null
  : .[0] xs

source Py from "main.py" ("default")
default :: a -> ?a -> a

testSafeHead :: ?Int
testSafeHead = safeHead [10, 20, 30]

testSafeHeadEmpty :: ?Int
testSafeHeadEmpty = safeHead []

testFromNull :: Int
testFromNull = default 0 Null
----

The Python side handles `None` the way Python always does:

[source, python]
.main.py
----
def default(default_val, x):
    if x is None:
        return default_val
    return x
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./main testSafeHead
10
$ ./main testSafeHeadEmpty

$ ./main --keep-null testSafeHeadEmpty
null
$ ./main testFromNull
0
----

NOTE: When an exported function's top-level result is `Null` (or `()`), the
nexus prints an empty line rather than the literal `null`. Printing `null` would
be noisy in a CLI tool, and a downstream consumer that ingested a stray `null`
line could choke on it or, worse, treat it as a valid record. Pass `--keep-null`
when you want the literal emitted, as above.

The same shape works in the other languages. In {cpp}, using `std::optional`:

[source, cpp]
----
#include <optional>

template <class T>
T orDefault(T default_val, const std::optional<T>& x) {
  if(x.has_value()){
    return x.value();
  } else {
    return default_val;
  }
}
----

NOTE: The helper is `orDefault`, not `default`. `default` is a {cpp} keyword,
and a function so named will not compile.

And in R, using `NULL`:

[source, r]
----
orDefault <- function(default_val, x){
  if(is.null(x)){
    return(default_val)
  } else {
    return(x)
  }
}
----

==== Optional record fields

Record fields may be optional, which is what you want for data with missing or
unknown values. The `where` form below is an alternative syntax for record
declarations, equivalent to the brace syntax in <<_records>>:

[source, morloc]
----
record Person where
  name :: Str
  age :: ?Int

record Py => Person = "dict"

source Py from "foo.py" ("makePerson")
makePerson :: Str -> ?Int -> Person

alice :: Person
alice = makePerson "Alice" 30

bob :: Person
bob = makePerson "Bob" Null
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./person alice
{"name":"Alice","age":30}
$ ./person bob
{"name":"Bob","age":null}
----

==== Optionals across languages

An optional produced in one language can be consumed in another with no interop
code from you:

[source, morloc]
----
-- C++ produces an optional value
source Cpp from "foo.hpp" ("cSafeDiv")
cSafeDiv :: Int -> Int -> ?Int

-- Python consumes it
source Py from "foo.py" ("pFromNull")
pFromNull :: Int -> ?Int -> Int

testCppToPy :: Int
testCppToPy = pFromNull (-1) (cSafeDiv 10 3)

testCppToPyNull :: Int
testCppToPyNull = pFromNull (-1) (cSafeDiv 10 0)
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./optional testCppToPy
3
$ ./optional testCppToPyNull
-1
----

The compiler generates the serialization at each boundary. A `std::nullopt` in
{cpp} becomes JSON `null`, which Python reads as `None`.

==== Implicit coercion

Morloc coerces a plain value to an optional wherever the context wants one, so
you never write a wrapper at the call site:

[source, morloc]
----
source Py from "foo.py" ("addOpt")
addOpt :: ?Int -> ?Int -> ?Int

-- both arguments are plain Int, coerced to ?Int
testCoerceAddOpt :: ?Int
testCoerceAddOpt = addOpt 3 4

-- the second argument (42) is Int, coerced to ?Int
testCoerceArg :: Int
testCoerceArg = pFromNull 0 42
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./optional testCoerceAddOpt
7
$ ./optional testCoerceArg
42
----

Coercion crosses language boundaries too. A {cpp} function returning a plain
`Int` can feed a Python parameter typed `?Int`:

[source, morloc]
----
source Cpp from "foo.hpp" ("cAddOne")
cAddOne :: Int -> Int

testCppIntToPyOpt :: Int
testCppIntToPyOpt = pFromNull 0 (cAddOne 41)
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./optional testCppIntToPyOpt
42
----

==== Nested optionals are idempotent

`?(?T)` parses and typechecks, but at run time it collapses to a single `?T`.
There is one `Null`, and no way to tell an "outer Null" from an "inner Null".
This is deliberate.

The reason goes back to why `?` is a language primitive rather than a library
type like {cpp}'s `std::optional`. `?` must lower to each target language's own
missing value: `None` in Python, `NULL` in R, `std::optional<T>` in {cpp}. In
Python and R -- and in most dynamic languages -- that value is structureless.
There is no mechanism for telling an outer `None` from an inner one; both are
the same singleton. If Morloc allowed two distinguishable null levels, the
semantics would diverge across backends, since {cpp} could fake it with nested
`std::optional` and Python could not. That would break the portability `?`
exists to provide.

So `?T`, `?(?T)`, and `?(?(?T))` all serialize to the same wire format and the
same runtime representation in every backend:

[source, morloc]
----
collapsed1 :: ?(?Int)
collapsed1 = Null

collapsed2 :: ?(?Int)
collapsed2 = 7   -- treated identically to (7 :: ?Int)
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./optional collapsed1

$ ./optional collapsed2
7
----

If you genuinely need layered nullability -- telling "the lookup failed" apart
from "the lookup succeeded but the field was unset" -- encode the distinction in
a type of your own:

[source, morloc]
----
record LookupResult = LookupResult
  { tableMissing :: Bool   -- step 1 failure
  , fieldMissing :: Bool   -- step 2 failure
  , value :: ?Int          -- present when both succeeded
  }
----

NOTE: Sum types -- tagged unions such as `data Result = Found Int | Missing` --
are planned but not yet supported. Their cross-language design is the open
problem, since not every backend has a first-class sum representation.

